Description: Anchor X11 dock windows to output edges, like i3
 X11 panels and docks (polybar, tint2, conky in dock mode) set
 _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE_DOCK. In the i3 session they sit at an edge of the
 screen and the windows around them get the rest of it. Under sway's
 Xwayland they were tiled like any window, decorated, moved with
 workspaces and took the keyboard focus when they appeared.
 .
 A non-override-redirect window of type _NET_WM_WINDOW_TYPE_DOCK is now
 no view: it spans the width of the output containing its requested
 position (or the focused output) against the top or bottom edge, never
 joins a workspace, gets no decorations and never takes the focus;
 pointer input still reaches it. The edge comes from
 _NET_WM_STRUT_PARTIAL when it reserves exactly one of them, otherwise
 from the requested position relative to the middle of the output.
 Docks are arranged after the exclusive zones of layer-shell surfaces
 (so next to swaybar), in mapping order; the usable area shrinks by
 their height, clamped to what is left of the output. Fullscreen views
 cover them. Height and strut changes are followed, docks move to the
 first remaining output when theirs is disabled, and windows that become
 or stop being docks while mapped are converted.
 .
 The upstream commit applies to 1.11 with two context-only conflicts
 (the image capture surface of master's views, and master's
 destroy_layers() call in output_disable()).
